{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_dvywoxycbnq4cbngn7fyfliwl232rlqs2scmqqfn7ym4rn5jk45q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## NO DIVERGENCES FOUND\n\nI read every line of the five C files against the three Rust files and then confirmed the reading with a bit-exact differential test. No behavioral divergence found. No files were edited (the only side effect is that `translation/target/release/libsphincs_core_det.so` now corresponds to the last feature set I built, `sha2,simple,256f`; cargo will rebuild it on demand).\n\n### What I verified by reading\n\nConstants / tables (machine-compared, not eyeballed):\n- All 64 `F_32` round constants in C order == `K_256` (`sha2.c:182-254` vs `backend/sha2.rs:132-141`); all 80 `F_64` constants == `K_512` (`sha2.c:331-422` vs `sha2.rs:145-226`).\n- `iv_256` / `iv_512` byte-identical to `IV_256` / `IV_512` (`sha2.c:459-473` vs `sha2.rs:412-422`).\n- `SPX_SHA256_BLOCK_BYTES=64`, `SPX_SHA256_OUTPUT_BYTES=32`, `SPX_SHA512_BLOCK_BYTES=128`, `SPX_SHA512_OUTPUT_BYTES=64`, `SPX_SHA256_ADDR_BYTES=22` all match (`sha2.h:7-17` vs `sha2.rs:14-21`).\n\nMessage schedule: the C `EXPAND_32` / `EXPAND_64` macro (16 sequential in-place register updates, applied 3x / 4x) is *not* literally the same code as the Rust `for i in 16..64 { W[i] = s1(W[i-2])+W[i-7]+s0(W[i-15])+W[i-16] }`. I proved equivalence by symbolic/numeric simulation of both forms for both widths — the 64-word and 80-word sequences are identical. Rotation amounts, `SHR` shifts (3/10/7/6), and `Sigma`/`sigma` amounts all match; `wrapping_add` correctly models C unsigned wraparound.\n\nPadding / finalize (the highest-risk area) — exact bound-by-bound match:\n- `sha256_inc_finalize`: `inlen &= 63`, `padded[inlen]=0x80`, `inlen<56` branch zero-fills `inlen+1..56`, length bytes at 56..64 with shifts 53/45/37/29/21/13/5 and `<<3`, hashes 64; else zero-fills `inlen+1..120`, length at 120..128, hashes 128 (`sha2.c:511-557` vs `sha2.rs:469-515`).\n- `sha512_inc_finalize`: `inlen &= 127`, `inlen<112` branch zero-fills `inlen+1..119`, 9 length bytes at 119..128 (shift 61 first), hashes 128; else `inlen+1..247`, length at 247..256, hashes 256 (`sha2.c:559-606` vs `sha2.rs:518-566`). Note the asymmetric `119`/`247` (not 120/248) is faithfully reproduced.\n- Every byte of `padded` that is later read is explicitly written in both, so the Rust zero-init of `padded` is unobservable.\n- `bytes = load_bigendian_64(state+32/64) + inlen` uses `wrapping_add`, matching `uint64_t` wraparound.\n\nConditional compilation:\n- `#if SPX_N >= 24` in `hash_sha2.c:11-27` → `const USE_512: bool = SPX_N >= 24` in `hash_sha2.rs:20`, driving `SPX_SHAX_OUTPUT_BYTES`, `SPX_SHAX_BLOCK_BYTES`, `shax*` and `mgf1_x`. `state[8 + SPX_SHAX_OUTPUT_BYTES]` → `SPX_SHAX_STATE_BYTES` (40/72).\n- `#if SPX_SHA512` in `sha2.c:697-700` (`seed_state`) → `#[cfg(spx_big_hash)]` at `sha2.rs:694`; `build.rs` emits `spx_big_hash` exactly for 192*/256*, and `context.rs` gates `state_seeded_512` with `all(feature=\"sha2\", spx_big_hash)`. Consistent, and I confirmed the resulting struct layout empirically (see below).\n- `#if SPX_SHA512 { if (inblocks > 1) thash_512(...) }` in both `thash_sha2_robust.c:22-27` and `thash_sha2_simple.c:22-27` → `if SPX_SHA512 == 1 && inblocks > 1` (`thash_sha2.rs:51`, `:179`). `params.rs:82` `SPX_SHA512 = SPX_BIG_HASH` equals the C `SPX_SHA512` for every sha2 parameter set (checked against `c_src/app/params/params-sphincs-sha2-*.h`).\n\n`SPX_SHA256_ADDR_BYTES` (22) truncation: `memcpy(buf, addr, 22)` / `memcpy(buf+SPX_N, addr, 22)` in `hash_sha2.c:51`, `thash_sha2_robust.c:35,61`, `thash_sha2_simple.c:36,54` → `&addr_bytes(addr)[..SPX_SHA256_ADDR_BYTES]` at `hash_sha2.rs:101`, `thash_sha2.rs:83,132,202,234`. `addr_bytes` is a native-endian `[u32;8]`→`[u8;32]` reinterpret, exactly `(unsigned char*)addr`. The oversized robust-256 `buf` (`SPX_N + 32 + n`, of which only `SPX_N + 22 + n` is used) is reproduced verbatim.\n\nmgf1 loop bounds: `for (i=0; (i+1)*OUT <= outlen; i++)` and `if (outlen > i*OUT) { … memcpy(out, outbuf, outlen - i*OUT) }` (`sha2.c:638-648`, `664-674`) → `sha2.rs:620-632`, `657-669`, including `u32_to_bytes(inbuf+inlen, i)` truncating the `unsigned long` counter to 32 bits (`i as u32`).\n\nFFI buffer lengths in the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers — each checked against what the C actually touches:\n| wrapper | Rust length | C access |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sha256_inc_init` | state 40 | writes `state[0..40]` |\n| `sha256_inc_blocks` | state 40, in `64*inblocks` | reads exactly `64*inblocks` |\n| `sha256_inc_finalize` | out 32, state 40, in `inlen` | writes `out[0..32]` |\n| `sha256` | out 32 | writes 32 |\n| `sha512_inc_init` | state 72 | writes `state[0..72]` |\n| `sha512_inc_blocks` | state 72, in `128*inblocks` | reads exactly `128*inblocks` |\n| `sha512_inc_finalize` | out 64, state 72, in `inlen` | writes `out[0..64]` |\n| `sha512` | out 64 | writes 64 |\n| `SPX_mgf1_256/512` | out `outlen`, in `inlen` | same |\n| `SPX_prf_addr` | out `SPX_N` | `memcpy(out, outbuf, SPX_N)` |\n| `SPX_gen_message_random` | r `SPX_N`, sk_prf `SPX_N`, optrand `SPX_N`, m `mlen` | same |\n| `SPX_hash_message` | digest `SPX_FORS_MSG_BYTES`, r `SPX_N`, pk `SPX_PK_BYTES`, m `mlen` | same |\n| `SPX_thash` | out `SPX_N`, in `inblocks*SPX_N` | same |\n\nInteger widths: `size_t`→`usize`, `unsigned long long`→`u64`, `unsigned int inblocks`→`c_uint`, `unsigned long outlen/inlen`→`u64` (identical on the LP64 target; the only ABI caveat is that `unsigned long` would be 32-bit on ILP32/LLP64, which this build never targets). `crypto_hashblocks_sha512`'s C `int` return truncation is dead (return value unused in both).\n\nAlso checked: `SPX_INBLOCKS` (`& -BLOCK` vs `& !(BLOCK-1)`), `TREE_MASK`/`LEAF_MASK` guards vs `(~0)>>(64-bits)`, `SPX_D == 1` branch, in-place `shaX(buf, buf, …)` aliasing in `gen_message_random`, and the VLA→stack/heap replacements in `mgf1_*` and `thash*` (allocation strategy only; no observable effect).\n\n### Empirical confirmation\n\nI built a `dlopen`-based differential harness (temporary, since removed) that loads the already-built C reference `cbuild/sha2-{robust,simple}-{128s,128f,192s,192f,256s,256f}/libcsphincs_all.so` (confirmed newer than every file in `c_src/`) alongside a freshly built Rust cdylib for the matching features, resolves every symbol per-handle with `RTLD_LOCAL` (verified the two address sets are distinct), and compares:\n\n- `sha256/512_inc_init` states; `inc_blocks` for 0..4 blocks from a random 40/72-byte state; `inc_finalize` out **and** resulting state for every `inlen` in 0..600 with randomized incoming byte counters; `sha256`/`sha512` for every `inlen` in 0..600.\n- `SPX_mgf1_256`/`SPX_mgf1_512` over `inlen` 0..520 × `outlen` 0..400 (covering `outlen == 0`, partial final blocks, and the Rust heap fallback above `MGF1_INBUF_CAP = 192`), comparing the whole 512-byte destination so any over-write would show.\n- `SPX_seed_state` comparing the full `spx_ctx` image (this is what pins the struct layout, including `state_seeded_512` at offset `2*SPX_N+40`).\n- `SPX_prf_addr`, `SPX_gen_message_random` (mlen 0..1000, both branches of the block-fill), `SPX_hash_message` (digest + `*tree` + `*leaf_idx`, mlen 0..1000, both branches), `SPX_thash` (inblocks 0..max(FORS_TREES,WOTS_LEN)+2, i.e. both the `inblocks==1` SHA-256 path, the `inblocks>1` SHA-512 path, `inblocks==0`, and the heap fallback), also asserting `addr` is left unmodified.\n\nResult: **0 differences in all 12 sha2 configurations**, and `nm -D` symbol parity is complete for all 12. A negative control (C 192f vs Rust 192s) correctly reported differences, so the harness is sensitive.\n\nRelevant paths: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-JZe0gx/c_src/lib/sha2/src/{sha2.c,hash_sha2.c,thash_sha2_robust.c,thash_sha2_simple.c}`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-JZe0gx/c_src/lib/sha2/include/sha2.h`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-JZe0gx/translation/src/backend/{sha2.rs,hash_sha2.rs,thash_sha2.rs}`, plus the supporting `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-JZe0gx/translation/{build.rs,src/params.rs,src/context.rs,src/address.rs,src/utils.rs}`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2675,"cache_read_input_tokens":121513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2675,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01FLLY3ipeDMFqC3FLTBAHag","session_id":"ea246c20-3a08-49a0-aeed-8ef41fe5c703","uuid":"f18daef8-b6aa-42fd-929c-e969e293a532","timestamp":"2026-09-02T13:33:55.856Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review sha2 backend translation"}
