{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mdtmt3tikefdrbovrd4bs6ubm2jhcqovoawg67gf3leyryclpjfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## NO DIVERGENCES FOUND\n\nI read both sides line-by-line and then confirmed the reading with a differential harness (`dlopen` both `.so`s, compare every shared export byte-for-byte). No behavioral divergence found in any of the six Rust files against the C ground truth.\n\n### What I verified by reading\n\n**`fips202.c` → `backend/fips202.rs`**\n- All 24 `KeccakF_RoundConstants` compared numerically (script) — identical.\n- `KeccakF1600_StatePermute`: extracted all 174 assignment statements from both sides, normalized, and compared in order — every rotate amount, every `~x & y` term, every `state[i]` index, the `round += 2` stride and `[round]`/`[round+1]` constant indices are identical. `rol()` is a true 64-bit rotate and all call sites use offsets in 1..=62.\n- `keccak_absorb`: `t[200]`, zero-fill `0..r`, `t[mlen] = p` (C's `t[i]=p` where `i==mlen` post-loop), `t[r-1] |= 128`, `r/8` load64 loop.\n- `keccak_squeezeblocks`: `r >> 3` store64 loop, `h += r` stride.\n- `keccak_inc_init`/`absorb`/`finalize`/`squeeze`: `s_inc[25]` counter word (26-word layout), `(s_inc[25]+i)>>3` / `<<(8*((s_inc[25]+i)&7))` little-endian byte-into-lane indexing, `r - s_inc[25]` computed in 64-bit before the `s_inc[25]=0` store, `(r-1)>>3` / `128 << (8*((r-1)&7))` pad, `s_inc[25] = r - i` after each squeezed block.\n- Rate constants: `SHAKE128_RATE 168`, `SHAKE256_RATE 136`, `SHA3_256_RATE 136`, `SHA3_512_RATE 72` — all match `fips202.h`. Domain separators `0x1F` (SHAKE) / `0x06` (SHA3).\n\n**`hash_shake.c` → `backend/hash_shake.rs`** — `prf_addr` buffer layout (`pub_seed‖addr‖sk_seed`, `2*SPX_N+SPX_ADDR_BYTES`), `gen_message_random` absorb order, `hash_message` absorbing the **full** `SPX_PK_BYTES` (contrast with haraka), digest/tree/leaf split, and the `>> (64-SPX_TREE_BITS)` / `>> (32-SPX_LEAF_BITS)` masks (checked for all six secpars, incl. 256f where `SPX_TREE_BITS == 64` and the shift is 0).\n\n**`thash_shake_{robust,simple}.c` → `backend/thash_shake.rs`** — buffer layout `pub_seed‖addr‖(in⊕bitmask)`, bitmask length `inblocks*SPX_N`, both `shake256` lengths.\n\n**`haraka.c` → `backend/haraka.rs`**\n- All 80 `haraka512_rc64` constants compared numerically — identical.\n- `br_aes_ct64_bitslice_Sbox`: all 132 statements diffed mechanically — identical (and I confirmed the C 32-bit S-box is statement-identical to the 64-bit one, so the single Rust macro is correct for both).\n- `br_aes_ct_ortho` / `br_aes_ct64_ortho` SWAP2/4/8 masks and shifts, `add_round_key{,32}`, `shift_rows{,32}`, `mix_columns{,32}`, `rotr16`/`rotr32`, `br_aes_ct64_interleave_in`/`_out`, `br_dec32le`/`br_enc32le`/`br_range_*` — all diffed mechanically, identical.\n- `haraka512_perm` mix-states: all 13 mask/shift terms identical; `tweaked512_rc64[2*i+j]` indexing correct. `haraka256` mix-states: all 7 terms identical; `tweaked256_rc32[2*i+j]` correct.\n- `tweak_constants`: 640-byte copy of the untweaked constants first, `haraka_S(buf, 640, pub_seed, SPX_N)`, then the `32*i` (256-bit) / `64*i` (512-bit) strides. `interleave_constant` does not read `out` in C, so the Rust fresh-zeroed temp is equivalent.\n- `HARAKAS_RATE 32`, `s_inc[65]` layout with the byte counter at `s_inc[64]`, `haraka_S_inc_absorb`'s `HARAKAS_RATE - s_inc[64]` computed before zeroing, the `uint8_t` `+=`/`-=` wrapping, `haraka_S_inc_finalize`'s `^= 0x1F` / `^= 128` at `HARAKAS_RATE-1`.\n- `haraka512` truncation `buf+8, buf+24, buf+32, buf+48` → `out+0,8,16,24`.\n\n**`hash_haraka.c` → `backend/hash_haraka.rs`** — `prf_addr`'s zeroed 64-byte `buf` (`addr‖sk_seed`), and crucially `hash_message` absorbing only `pk + SPX_N` for `SPX_N` bytes (root part only), not the full PK.\n\n**`thash_haraka_{robust,simple}.c` → `backend/thash_haraka.rs`** — the `inblocks == 1` F-function branch (zeroed 64-byte `buf_tmp`, 32-byte addr copy, `haraka256`→XOR→`haraka512`) vs. the `haraka_S` branch, and the address-only (no `pub_seed`) buffer prefix.\n\n**FFI byte lengths** — every `from_raw_parts{,_mut}` length in the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers matches what C reads/writes: `st(s,25)` for the non-incremental Keccak state, `st(s_inc,26)` for the incremental one, `65` for haraka's `s_inc`, `nblocks*RATE` for squeezeblocks, `32`/`64` for the sha3 finalizers, `SPX_N` for `out`/`R`, `SPX_FORS_MSG_BYTES` for `digest`, `SPX_PK_BYTES`(=`2*SPX_N`) for `pk` in both `SPX_hash_message`, `inblocks*SPX_N` for `thash`'s input, `64`/`64`/`32`/`32`/`32`/`32` for `haraka512_perm`/`haraka512`/`haraka256`. `unsigned long long` params are `u64`, `size_t` params are `usize` — matching the C prototypes in `fips202.h`, `haraka.h`, `hash.h`, `thash.h`.\n\n**Address offsets** — `params.rs` `offsets` (non-sha2 arm: 3, 8, 19, 20, 27, 31, 27, 28) matches `shake_offsets.h` and `haraka_offsets.h` exactly. `SPX_SHAKE`/`SPX_HARAKA` are only used in C as `#ifdef` selectors for `spx_ctx`'s extra fields; `context.rs` reproduces that with cfg-gated `tweaked512_rc64: [[u64;8];10]` + `tweaked256_rc32: [[u32;8];10]` in the correct order for the haraka arm, and no extra fields for shake.\n\n### Differential testing (empirical confirmation)\n\n- `fips202`: 3000 iterations × (`shake256`, `shake256_absorb`+`shake256_squeezeblocks` incl. resulting state, multi-call `shake256_inc_absorb`/`finalize`/`squeeze` incl. the 26-word state after every call) — 0 mismatches. Plus exhaustive `{0,1,135,136,137,271,272,273}²` in/out length grid and the absorb-exactly-one-block case.\n- SPX level: 3000 iterations of `SPX_initialize_hash_function` (full 2 KB ctx compared → confirms identical `spx_ctx` layout **and** identical tweaked constants), `SPX_prf_addr`, `SPX_gen_message_random`, `SPX_hash_message` (digest + `*tree` + `*leaf_idx`), and `SPX_thash` for **every** `inblocks` from 1 to `max(SPX_WOTS_LEN, SPX_FORS_TREES)` plus the three aliasing patterns C actually uses (`thash(b,b,1)`, `thash(b+N,b,2)`, `thash(b,b,2)`). Run for all 24 combinations of {haraka, shake} × {robust, simple} × {128s,128f,192s,192f,256s,256f} — 0 mismatches everywhere.\n- haraka internals: 2000 iterations of `SPX_haraka512_perm` (incl. the `out == in` aliasing call), `SPX_haraka512`, `SPX_haraka256`, `SPX_haraka_S` over a 9×9 in/out length grid `{0,1,31,32,33,63,64,65,640}`, and `SPX_haraka_S_inc_*` with absorb lengths 0..69 and multi-call partial squeezes (exercises the `s_inc[64]` leftover-byte path) — 0 mismatches for 128s/192s/256s/128f.\n\n### Non-behavioral notes (not divergences, but worth knowing)\n\n1. `c_src/lib/shake/src/fips202.c` **does not define** `shake128*`, `sha3_256*`, or `sha3_512*` (they are only declared in `fips202.h`; `nm` on the C `.so` confirms they are absent). `translation/src/backend/fips202.rs:512-560,633-703` implements them and `hash_shake.rs:141-260` exports all 14 as `#[no_mangle]`. They match upstream PQClean and are unreachable, so there is nothing to diverge from — but the Rust `.so` exports 14 global symbols the C library does not.\n2. `thash_haraka.rs:135-136` (and `thash_sha2.rs:261-262`, `thash_blake.rs:163-164`) build an aliasing `&mut [u8]`/`&[u8]` pair when C calls `thash(out, out, ...)`. `thash_shake.rs:106-109` avoids this by copying the input into a local first. Currently benign (all writes to `out` happen after the last read of `input` in every branch), but it is UB and the inconsistency is a latent hazard.\n3. `haraka.rs:897-983` uses `core::slice::from_raw_parts{,_mut}` directly, whereas `hash_shake.rs:116-131` wraps them in `sl`/`sl_mut` helpers that special-case `len == 0` to avoid forming a slice from a possibly-null pointer. `SPX_haraka_S_inc_absorb`/`SPX_gen_message_random` can legitimately be called with `mlen == 0`.\n\nNote on workspace state: I ran one `cargo build` into `translation/target/release` (shake,robust,128s) before switching to an isolated `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`; a `run_all.sh` appears to be running concurrently and rebuilds that path per combination, so this is transient. All scratch files I created were removed. No files under `c_src/` or `translation/` were modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2509,"cache_read_input_tokens":181271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2509,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01KsN8KTe8GxcDs8FuWWEDTY","session_id":"ea246c20-3a08-49a0-aeed-8ef41fe5c703","uuid":"91a0bcf8-85d7-4d4c-b0b1-64e04f345481","timestamp":"2026-09-02T13:39:48.080Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review shake+haraka backend translation"}
